BLOCKS = [
    ('chapter', 'Before you begin', 'How to use this Guidebook',
     'The DM Guide tells you how the game works. This book tells you how to tell the story.'),
    ('p', 'You already have the <b>DM Guide</b>: the rules, every button on the GM screen, the puzzles and the finale. '
          'This Guidebook sits beside it and does a different job. It walks you through the first half of the week, '
          'from the owl at the window to the end of Day 4, one scene at a time. For every scene it tells you where everyone is, '
          'what to read out, what each person says and how they say it, and what happens when your players do something nobody planned for.'),
    ('p', 'Your players test everything. They will open every barrel, lift every rug, tap every wall and try Alohomora on the Headmaster’s hat. '
          'This book is written for exactly that table. Part 1 gives you a system for answering any poke in a second. '
          'Part 5 goes room by room through every location picture, object by object.'),
    ('h3', 'The boxes in this book'),
    ('read', 'Parchment boxes are read aloud, as written or in your own words.'),
    ('say', 'Someone in the castle', '“Purple boxes are a character speaking. Read them in their voice. Each character’s voice is described in Part 2.”'),
    ('wait', 'Red boxes mean stop and let the players talk.'),
    ('gm', 'Teal boxes say what to press in the app.'),
    ('tip', 'Gold boxes are advice you can take or leave.'),
    ('ifs', [('If they…', 'Tables like this one list what the players might do in a scene, and what happens when they do.')]),
    ('h3', 'How the book is laid out'),
    ('table', ['Part', 'What it is for', 'When to read it'], [
        ['1. The poking table', 'A system for answering any “I look under the…” in one breath.', 'Before the first session. Keep the quick-reference page handy.'],
        ['2. The cast', 'How to play every person in the castle: voice, body, secrets, reactions.', 'Before the first session, then dip in.'],
        ['3. The Prologue', 'Letters to the Sorting, scene by scene.', 'At the table, in order.'],
        ['4. Days 1 to 4', 'Every block of every day: who is where, what appears, what to say.', 'At the table, in order.'],
        ['5. The castle, room by room', 'Every object in every picture, and what poking it does.', 'Whenever someone says “I search the…”.'],
        ['6. The mystery so far', 'Every clue up to Day 4, where it is, and how to handle accusations.', 'Between sessions.'],
        ['7. The Quest Guide', 'Getting players to bite, and every quest in depth. Also printed on its own.', 'Before the first session.'],
    ], [34, 82, 54]),
    ('tip', 'You do not need to memorise any of this. Read Parts 1 and 2 once. Then read each scene a minute before it happens, while the players are busy with their phones.'),

    ('chapter', 'Part 1', 'The poking table', 'Your players will touch everything. Here is how to make every touch worth it.'),
    ('h2', 'The golden rule'),
    ('p', 'When a player pokes at something, they are telling you what they find interesting. Reward that. '
          'Every poke gets <b>something</b>: a sight, a smell, a sound, a small find, or a consequence. '
          'The only wrong answer is a flat “there’s nothing there”. Even an empty barrel smells of old apples and has a name scratched inside the lid.'),
    ('p', 'But not every poke gets <i>loot</i>. If every barrel holds a Galleon, the barrels stop being interesting by Day 2. '
          'The trick is to make most pokes give <b>flavour</b>, some give <b>a small find</b>, and a few give <b>something that matters</b>.'),
    ('h2', 'The three answers'),
    ('table', ['Answer', 'How often', 'What it sounds like'], [
        ['<b>Flavour</b>', 'Most of the time', 'A detail that makes the place feel real, or a joke. “Inside the barrel: pickled onions, and one of them is looking at you.”'],
        ['<b>A small find</b>', 'About one poke in four, and at most once per place per day', 'A coin, a sweet, a note, a useful hint. Roll on the Pocket Finds table below, or pick.'],
        ['<b>Something that matters</b>', 'Only where this book says so', 'A clue, a quest item, a secret passage, a chest. These are placed by the app or by you, and Part 5 says where.'],
    ], [34, 46, 90]),
    ('tip', 'A good habit: when a player pokes something for the first time in a place, give them a little more than flavour. When they poke the same kind of thing for the fifth time, give them a joke. The joke is the reward for persistence.'),

    ('h2', 'The poke ladder'),
    ('p', 'Every investigation climbs the same three rungs. Decide which rung they are on, and you know what to do.'),
    ('table', ['Rung', 'What the player says', 'What you do'], [
        ['<b>1. Look</b>', '“What’s in the corner?” “Is there anything on the desk?”', 'No roll. Describe what anyone would see. Mention one thing that invites a closer look.'],
        ['<b>2. Search</b>', '“I open the barrel.” “I look behind the painting.” “I tap the walls.”', 'Roll if there is something to find or a reason it could go wrong. Usually INT (searching), WIS (noticing) or DEX (doing it quietly).'],
        ['<b>3. Force</b>', '“I smash the crate.” “I cast Alohomora.” “I climb the shelves.”', 'Roll, and there is always a risk: noise, damage, a teacher, lost house points. Say the risk before they roll.'],
    ], [26, 64, 80]),
    ('h3', 'Picking the number'),
    ('table', ['How hard', 'DC', 'Example'], [
        ['Easy', '8', 'Finding the pear on the fruit painting once they know to look'],
        ['Normal', '12', 'Searching a cluttered desk, noticing a portrait pretending to sleep'],
        ['Hard', '15', 'Finding a hidden catch on a bookcase, sneaking past Filch'],
        ['Very hard', '18', 'Picking an old castle lock with a hairpin, lying to the Headmaster'],
    ], [30, 16, 124]),
    ('p', 'Players roll on the Dice tab of their phones and tell you the total. A natural 20 always gives something extra. A natural 1 always gives something funny, never something cruel.'),
    ('tip', 'Before a Force roll, always say the risk out loud: “You can smash it, but it will be loud. Filch is two corridors away.” Then let them choose. The best moments come from players deciding the risk is worth it.'),

    ('h2', 'Common things, and what is in them'),
    ('p', 'Use this table for anything not listed in Part 5. Roll a d6 or pick the one that fits the room.'),
    ('table', ['Thing', '1–2: flavour', '3–4: a little more', '5–6: a small find'], [
        ['Barrel', 'Rainwater and a drowned leaf', 'Pickled onions. One blinks.', 'A cork with a Galleon pushed into it (+1 Galleon)'],
        ['Crate or box', 'Straw and the smell of apples', 'Old school robes, moth-eaten, with a name tag too faded to read', 'Pumpkin Pasty, wrapped in paper'],
        ['Cupboard or wardrobe', 'Coats that smell of rain', 'A broom that tries to sweep them out', 'A spare Pepperup Potion behind the brooms'],
        ['Under a bed or table', 'Dust bunnies that scatter like mice', 'A sock, a quill, a note: “meet me at the lake”', 'A Chocolate Frog (the card is a common one)'],
        ['Behind a painting', 'Bare stone and a draught', 'The painting’s back is signed and dated a hundred years ago', 'A gap in the stone: a hidden passage (see Hidden passages)'],
        ['Tapping the walls', 'Solid stone, cold under the knuckles', 'A hollow sound. A loose stone with a sweet wrapper behind it', 'A hollow sound and a draught: a hidden passage'],
        ['A rug', 'Floorboards and a lost button', 'A scorch mark shaped like a hand', 'A trapdoor that is nailed shut (fun to argue about)'],
        ['Fireplace', 'Warm ash', 'Soot falls on whoever leaned in. They sneeze.', 'A note half burnt: a scrap of someone’s homework, or a clue line from Part 6'],
        ['Bookshelf', 'Books, alphabetised by smell', 'A book bites, or sings, or sighs when opened', 'A bookmark that is a Chocolate Frog card'],
        ['Suit of armour', 'It creaks when you look at it', 'The visor snaps shut on fingers (1 HP)', '“You find Galleons in an old suit of armour.” +5 Galleons'],
        ['Statue', 'Cold stone, a smooth worn nose', 'It turns its head to watch them leave', 'A loose plinth with a hidden button: a passage'],
        ['Window', 'The grounds, the lake, the forest', 'An owl taps to be let in. It has nothing for them. It wants a treat.', 'Something moving at the edge of the forest at night'],
        ['Plants and pots', 'Earth and worms', 'The plant bites (1 HP), or sneezes pollen', 'A seed pod worth giving to Longbottom (+5 house points when they do)'],
        ['Cauldron', 'Something brown and bubbling', 'It smells of whatever they love most', 'Leftover potion: a Pepperup, if they dare'],
        ['Trunk', 'Robes and books', 'A lock with no keyhole', 'Could be a chest on the map. If not: a Remembrall, glowing red'],
    ], [30, 40, 48, 52]),
    ('gm', 'To hand over a find: Players tab, Give item (or the +Galleons button). Chests already on the map are opened by the players from the Here tab on their phones, with their own roll.'),

    ('h2', 'Pocket finds'),
    ('p', 'When a poke earns a small find and you want something quick, roll a d20.'),
    ('table', ['d20', 'Find', 'd20', 'Find'], [
        ['1', 'A note that just says “behind you”. Nobody is.', '11', 'Bertie Bott’s Beans (give the item; eating it spins the bean wheel)'],
        ['2', 'A Knut, a Sickle and a button (no game value, great to hold)', '12', 'A Chocolate Frog'],
        ['3', 'A love letter to a professor, unsigned', '13', 'A Pumpkin Pasty'],
        ['4', 'A Dungbomb (it goes off if dropped: everyone nearby rolls CON or gags)', '14', '+3 Galleons in a lost purse'],
        ['5', 'Filch’s list of banned items, very long', '15', 'A Pepperup Potion'],
        ['6', 'A self-inking quill that writes rude words', '16', 'A scrap with three runes and their letters pencilled under them (confirm those letters in the rune notebook)'],
        ['7', 'A Gobstone that complains', '17', 'Extendable Ears'],
        ['8', 'A cat hair. Mrs Norris was here.', '18', 'A Focus Draught'],
        ['9', 'A tiny portrait of a mouse, very chatty', '19', 'A Remembrall'],
        ['10', 'A torn page of someone’s Potions homework (marked: Troll)', '20', 'Felix Felicis, or a real clue from Part 6'],
    ], [12, 73, 12, 73]),
    ('tip', 'Rolls 16 and 20 are your levers. If the players are stuck on runes, use 16. If they are stuck on the mystery, use 20 to hand them a clue they missed.'),

    ('h2', 'Hidden passages'),
    ('p', 'Hogwarts is full of them, and finding one is the best feeling in the game. When a search finds a passage, decide where it goes. Good options:'),
    ('list', [
        '<b>A shortcut</b> to a place on the same floor. Next time they go there, they save a scene (tell them, and let them skip a corridor).',
        '<b>A way past Filch.</b> At Curfew, a known passage lets them skip one spin of Out after curfew.',
        '<b>A spy hole</b> into a room. Behind the portraits of the Grand Staircase, a passage has holes where a painted figure’s eyes should be. From there they can watch the Defence classroom, or the Great Hall, without being seen.',
        '<b>A dead end</b> with something in it: a skeleton of a broom, an old wrapper, initials carved by students fifty years ago.',
    ]),
    ('p', 'One rule: no passage leads into the Undercroft before Day 7, and none goes into the Restricted Section without the players paying for it somehow (a roll, a risk, a cost).'),
    ('gm', 'The Out after curfew wheel and Hogwarts surprises wheel both have a Secret / Passage result. When it lands, the passage is real: describe it and let them name it. Players love naming passages.'),

    ('h2', 'Spells on the world'),
    ('p', 'Once they have a wand, they will point it at everything. Say yes as often as you can. These are the spells they have by Day 4, and some they might know from home.'),
    ('table', ['Spell', 'On the world', 'Where it matters'], [
        ['Lumos', 'Light. Dark corners give up their secrets: footprints in dust, writing on a wall, eyes in the dark.', 'The Restricted Section at night, the Dungeons, the Forest. Devil’s Snare hates it.'],
        ['Alohomora', 'Opens ordinary locks: cupboards, classroom doors, desk drawers. (Taught on Day 5, so only those who knew it before.)', 'Very old locks ignore it: the Restricted Section gate, the serpent door, the Turning Lock.'],
        ['Wingardium Leviosa', 'Lifts things that are out of reach: a book from a high shelf, a key from a ledge.', 'The Trophy Room’s top shelf, the Library gallery.'],
        ['Accio', 'Summons a small visible object. Not people, not things in someone’s pocket, not things they can’t name.', 'Fetching the stolen jars from Peeves’s hiding place, if they have seen them.'],
        ['Reparo', 'Mends broken things. Lovely for apologising to Filch.', 'A smashed vase is +5 house points restored and one less enemy.'],
        ['Aguamenti', 'Water. Puts out fires, fills cauldrons, soaks Peeves.', 'The Greenhouses. Longbottom is grateful.'],
        ['Expelliarmus', 'Knocks a wand away. On a student, it starts a fight.', 'On a teacher: −10 points and detention, unless they had a very good reason.'],
        ['Incendio', 'Fire. Burns paper, cloth and wood; stone just gets warm. Taught on Day 7.', 'Paint burns. If they try it on a portrait before Day 7, the portrait screams and every portrait in the corridor goes quiet.'],
        ['Flipendo', 'A push. Knocks over barrels, opens heavy doors, startles owls.', 'Fun at the Owlery. Expect feathers.'],
        ['Finite Incantatem', 'Ends a spell. On an enchanted object it goes still.', 'On a portrait: the figure freezes for a minute, mid-sentence, like a paused film.'],
        ['Stupefy', 'Knocks someone out. The Sinclair starts with it.', 'Never quietly. Anyone who sees it tells a teacher.'],
    ], [30, 80, 60]),
    ('h3', 'The covered painting and spells'),
    ('p', 'Sooner or later someone will try to curse, burn, cut or summon the covered painting. Be ready:'),
    ('ifs', [
        ('Diffindo or a knife on the cloth', 'The cord parts. The cloth slips a hand’s width and stops, as if something caught it from the inside. Through the gap: a corner of gilded frame and the smell of turpentine.'),
        ('Incendio or any fire', 'The flame slides off the cloth like water off oil. The cloth is not even warm. Somewhere under it, someone laughs very softly.'),
        ('Accio', 'The painting does not move. The caster’s wand hand goes cold to the elbow for an hour. (No mechanical effect; describe it.)'),
        ('Pulling the cloth right off', 'They can’t. It is as heavy as a lead curtain. With a STR 16 check, it lifts for one second: they see a painted young man, beautiful and unfinished, the background still bare canvas. His eyes open. Give that student the Fear status for the rest of the scene.'),
        ('Talking to it', 'Before Day 5 it says almost nothing. One line, velvet-soft: “Not yet, child. Not yet.”'),
    ]),
    ('gm', 'Statuses are on the Table tab: tap a student, then choose from the status list (Fear, Stunned, Silenced and so on).'),

    ('h2', 'Breaking the rules'),
    ('p', 'They will. Hogwarts has a simple system for it, and it is also your best steering tool.'),
    ('table', ['They…', 'What happens'], [
        ['Are out after curfew', 'Spin Out after curfew for each group that moves (Wheels tab). Filch means detention.'],
        ['Get detention', 'Detention is in the Trophy Room with Filch, polishing cups. It starts the secret quest The Old Cup. Detention is a reward in disguise.'],
        ['Fight in a corridor', '−5 points each, unless a teacher is in the room, then −10 and detention.'],
        ['Curse a teacher', '−20 points and detention. If it is Ashgrove, he forgives them warmly and it is somehow worse.'],
        ['Steal from a classroom', 'If anyone sees: −10 points. If nobody sees: Portrait gossip wheel for the nearest portrait.'],
        ['Go into the Forest', 'Out of bounds always. −10 points if caught. Spin the Forbidden Forest wheel each visit.'],
        ['Lie to a teacher', 'CHA check against the teacher’s WIS: Filch 10, Grimsby 14, Vance 15, the Headmaster 18. Ashgrove believes everything, which should worry them.'],
    ], [40, 130]),
    ('tip', 'Points work best as theatre. Say the number like a teacher would, then hit the ⏳ Points button while everyone watches the hourglass on the TV. A table that has just lost twenty points will behave for exactly one block.'),

    ('h2', 'When they go somewhere early'),
    ('p', 'Players with a map will try to go everywhere on Day 1. Let them. The castle is ready for it.'),
    ('ifs', [
        ('The Forbidden Forest, Day 1', 'It is dark under the trees even at noon. Spin the Forest wheel. The centaur is there from Day 1 afternoon. Bramble is not findable until Scamander’s lesson on Day 3, but they can hear something large and frightened deep in the trees.'),
        ('The Restricted Section', 'It is secret on the map until they find the way in: a gate at the back of the Library, chained, with a lock that ignores Alohomora. A Hard (15) DEX roll with a hairpin, a very good lie to the librarian, or the Okafor’s Curse-Breaker gift gets them through. Inside before Day 3: chained books that whisper, nothing they need yet.'),
        ('The Room of Requirement', 'Walking past the blank wall three times, thinking of what they need, works any day. Before Day 5 the room is full of junk and one ancient chest (it is on the map from Day 1). The ticking chest of the Turning Lock is there too, but the three ring clues are scattered.'),
        ('The Undercroft', 'There is no way down until they find it: the stair is hidden behind the Dungeons fireplace. If they find it early (Hard WIS search of the fireplace, or the Marlowe asking a snake), the stair ends at the serpent door. The Marlowe’s key and Parseltongue open it, onto a long cold corridor where the covered painting hangs. The great inner chamber stays sealed until the seventh night: “The far door hums. It is not ready for you. Not yet.”'),
        ('Hogsmeade', 'Not until Day 6. The gates are locked and Filch is very pleased about it.'),
        ('A teacher’s office', 'Locked. Inside Grimsby’s: ingredients, a half-brewed cauldron (Day 5 clue), very bad poetry. Inside Ashgrove’s: more paintings than the classroom, all facing the wall. Turning one round is a WIS 12 check not to scream.'),
    ]),
]
